{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 RCA — confirmed test-oracle defect; production fail-closed semantics remain authoritative.\n\nMechanism: tests/unit/test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py::_fixture_batched_map retries the batched resolver up to five times, but each equivalence assertion then calls authorship.resolve_event_commit exactly once (lines 247 and the base sweep at 209). resolve_event_commit is an authorship/era proof resolver whose contract at src/rebar/attest/authorship_resolution.py:21-23 is deliberate: any git nonzero, no match, missing git, timeout, or exception returns None and never raises. Downstream live-event verification treats an unresolvable introducing commit as fail-closed (verify_authorship.py:243-257), so changing production to treat a transient as proof would be wrong. A bounded retry could preserve final fail-closedness, but rc=0/empty conflates a genuine no-match with a transient and each narrow history walk is documented at 6.78s on the live 71k-commit store; retrying production no-match would multiply the fallback cost without a reliable transient classifier.\n\nDiscriminating runtime reproduction used a real temporary tickets Git repo and real event commit. The batched map resolved f4d016a..., then a one-shot injected rc128 on only the per-event git-log made resolve_event_commit return None; an immediate identical second call returned f4d016a.... Thus equivalence was False on the first environmental read and True on replay, ruling out topology, parser, pathspec, and attribution defects. This exactly matches CI change 1837: batched side recovered, oracle side returned None.\n\nLegitimacy: defect in the test harness, not production behavior. Prior closed bug innovative-dandruffy-deer explicitly established the same boundary: fixture-only bounded recovery, production authorship fail-closed/fallback unchanged. There is therefore no unresolved design choice requiring escalation.\n\nCaller gate: production callers rely on None as fail-closed evidence and must not change. Repair is confined to the equivalence oracle: bounded exact retries for the per-event expected value, return only on a non-None SHA, and loud bounded diagnostics on persistent failure. Existing exact position-to-SHA assertions stay intact; no production source changes."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787026464840398001,"uuid":"749e1844-cc0e-413c-b35c-a1eb79fb2335"}